{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1228 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "05c4488640109a5799daaac2e2a9a9742601e745f4ab07ee471389a03912da52" :edb/db-before-t 1227 :edb/db-after-t 1228 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534540 50 #inst "2026-10-12T02:24:11.466Z" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186198533 1050 420.0 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186198788 1071 "![](images/83/83ebd6f5d742a869b267a0cead4a9a94ae17fa534ca3e1cfddbb495c67390ecf.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-x\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,2\\}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186198788 1071 "![](images/83/83ebd6f5d742a869b267a0cead4a9a94ae17fa534ca3e1cfddbb495c67390ecf.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-x\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,2}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186198788 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x$ and *below* the line $y=2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x,\\,y<2.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-x<y<2.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=-x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n-x\\le y<2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-x\\le y<2\\}.\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186198788 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x$ and *below* the line $y=2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x,\\,y<2.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-x<y<2.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=-x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n-x\\le y<2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-x\\le y<2}.\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186198790 1071 "![](images/fb/fbd778f48199acd5b731e739cb527a990ba28a8d88981d50554d135e853f8815.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$36$\n\n$\\}$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186198790 1071 "![](images/fb/fbd778f48199acd5b731e739cb527a990ba28a8d88981d50554d135e853f8815.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$36$\n\n$}$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186198790 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=3$ and vertical radius $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(0)^{2}+9(0)^{2}\\,<\\,36.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<36.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $<$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<36\\}.\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186198790 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=3$ and vertical radius $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(0)^{2}+9(0)^{2}\\,<\\,36.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<36.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $<$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<36}.\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186199551 1064 "We can use set-builder notation to represent sets that lie in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, let's describe the following shaded region using set-builder-notation.\n\n![](images/2c/2c4545ab59c1bf6847ab38b09ddf8b86c3227bbc6006835aceb8637b45bff817.png)\n\n  \n\nFirst, notice that a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2<x<6,\\,2<y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that we also need to   \n\n- *include* the left edge ($x=2$), and\n\n- *include* the right edge ($x=6$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbols to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le x\\le 6,\\,2<y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{\n(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2\n\\: : \\:  \n\n2 \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq} \nx \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq}  \n6, \n\\: \n2 \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt} \ny\n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt}   \n5 \n\\Big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186199551 1064 "We can use set-builder notation to represent sets that lie in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, let's describe the following shaded region using set-builder-notation.\n\n![](images/2c/2c4545ab59c1bf6847ab38b09ddf8b86c3227bbc6006835aceb8637b45bff817.png)\n\nFirst, notice that a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2<x<6,\\,2<y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that we also need to   \n\n- *include* the left edge ($x=2$), and\n\n- *include* the right edge ($x=6$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbols to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le x\\le 6,\\,2<y<5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb R, y\\in\\mathbb R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R = \\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{\n(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2\n\\: : \\:  \n\n2 \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq} \nx \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\leq}  \n6, \n\\: \n2 \n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt} \ny\n\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\lt}   \n5 \n\\Big\\}.\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186287728 1071 "![](images/70/7021cfd590d7e090128061d42c140ab81633f20e8cd1bff60f6252bc8e9aa882.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-{y}^{2}\\}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186287728 1071 "![](images/70/7021cfd590d7e090128061d42c140ab81633f20e8cd1bff60f6252bc8e9aa882.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-{y}^{2}}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186287728 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $x=-{y}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge -{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186287728 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $x=-{y}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge -{y}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186287766 1071 "![](images/a6/a659e2d14a645036488833cd920e6bb3e3d7a7716724dba63814be6df4066fe9.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\,{{field-1}}\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,6,\\,-1\\,{{field-3}}\\,y\\,{{field-4}}\\,4\\}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186287766 1071 "![](images/a6/a659e2d14a645036488833cd920e6bb3e3d7a7716724dba63814be6df4066fe9.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\,{{field-1}}\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,6,\\,-1\\,{{field-3}}\\,y\\,{{field-4}}\\,4}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186287766 1074 "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3<x<6,\\,-1<y<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to *include* the left edge ($x=3$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbol to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\le x<6,\\,-1<y<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\le x<6,\\,-1<y<4\\}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186287766 1074 "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3<x<6,\\,-1<y<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to *include* the left edge ($x=3$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbol to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\le x<6,\\,-1<y<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\le x<6,\\,-1<y<4}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186287785 1071 "![](images/34/34f5bb40fdcd4f71967ef2b1cc15328cf8d4d02cdd54b19a406273fae6c88ad4.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x-1\\}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186287785 1071 "![](images/34/34f5bb40fdcd4f71967ef2b1cc15328cf8d4d02cdd54b19a406273fae6c88ad4.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x-1}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186287785 1074 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x-1\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny>-x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y>-x-1\\}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186287785 1074 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x-1\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny>-x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y>-x-1}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186287790 1071 "![](images/77/7703b8c621b268a9fcbb412cd3a0617045145d475024dc91cecfa09a18fc8712.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-2\\}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186287790 1071 "![](images/77/7703b8c621b268a9fcbb412cd3a0617045145d475024dc91cecfa09a18fc8712.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-2}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186287790 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *right* the line $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-2\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $x=-2$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge -2\\}.\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [17592186287790 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *right* the line $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-2\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $x=-2$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge -2}.\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 false] [17592186374394 1071 "![](images/99/993cfa437e54f390237b85f3d7ee8a76b626d65431908fe039e0046ba0a33bb1.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\,{{field-1}}\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,6,\\,1\\,{{field-3}}\\,y\\,{{field-4}}\\,4\\}\n$$" 13194139534540 17592186045417 true] [